    "explanation": "In Lecture 3, we studied the Zero-Probability Problem: if a single feature category has zero counts in the training duplicate class, P(x_j | Duplicate) = 0. Because Naive Bayes multiplies class-conditional probabilities (\u220f P(x_i | Y)), that single zero wipes out all other 14 matching attributes, collapsing the posterior duplicate probability to 0.0. Laplace smoothing (alpha=1.0) adds a uniform pseudo-count across all bins, ensuring P(x_j | Duplicate) > 0 and preserving the overwhelming cumulative evidence."
  },
